## Vercel Speed Insights Installation Complete

### Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for this Next.js project following the latest official documentation from https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart.

### Changes Made

#### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` version ^2.1.1 using npm
- Package added to dependencies in package.json
- package-lock.json updated with all required dependencies

#### 2. Code Integration
**Modified: `src/app/layout.tsx`**
- Added import: `import { SpeedInsights } from '@vercel/speed-insights/next'`
- Added `<SpeedInsights />` component in the body section after the main content (AppLayout)
- Follows Next.js App Router pattern as documented in the official quickstart guide

### Implementation Details

The integration follows Vercel's recommended approach for Next.js App Router (v13.5+):
- Used the `/next` import path for optimal Next.js integration
- Placed the component at the root layout level to track all pages
- Positioned after the main content (children) as recommended

### Verification Steps Completed

✅ Dependencies installed successfully (npm install)
✅ Build completed successfully (npm run build)
✅ Linter verified - no new errors introduced
✅ TypeScript compilation passed
✅ All 11 routes built successfully

### Next Steps

1. **Deploy to Vercel**: The Speed Insights will automatically activate when deployed to Vercel
2. **Verification**: After deployment, check that the tracking script appears in the page's head tag
3. **Dashboard**: View performance metrics in the Vercel dashboard under Speed Insights

### Technical Notes

- Framework: Next.js 16.4.6 with App Router
- Package Manager: npm
- Installation Method: Framework-specific integration (not generic React)
- Component Type: Server-side compatible (no 'use client' directive needed)

The implementation is production-ready and will start collecting Speed Insights data once deployed to Vercel.
